  "query_preview": "..A large and persistent trough is still present, \ncurrently centered over the Pacific Northwest with a trough axis \nextending along the west coast. This has generally brought a drier\nsouthwest flow that has limited storm activity over the past few \ndays, and will continue to be an influence today. However, \nmoisture does begin to creep up from the south in far eastern AZ. \nThe result will be scattered showers and thunderstorms mainly \nacross the White Mountains and Eastern Mogollon Rim this \nafternoon/evening, with very isolated activity elsewhere. The west\ncoast trough will also keep us under a tight pressure gradient \naloft, and continued breezy southwest winds with gusts up to 35 \nmph are expected (especially towards western AZ). On Thursday \nevening/Friday morning, there is potential for storm activity to \ncontinue overnight across eastern AZ. This is a very uncertain \nsetup, and will be highly dependent on how convection evolves \nduring the day in southeastern AZ. Agreement remains low among \nCAMs, but the overall forecast calls for a 30-50% chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms during this period. Moisture continues \nto trend upwards on Friday, with similar placement and slightly \nhigher chances of storms. On Saturday and Sunday, the forecast \nremains in flux as the details of Hurricane Marie evolve. The \nwest coast trough will remain strong through this period, but \nmoisture from Marie begins to get wrapped into the southwest flow.\nMany ensemble members bring an enhanced surge of PW across \nwestern AZ, while eastern AZ continues to experience a moist southerly\nflow. The details still vary on the exact placement and timing of\nthe deepest moisture. Overall, the forecast calls for storm\nchances gradually increasing on Saturday then peaking on\nSunday/Monday. As we move into next week, thunderstorm chances \ncontinue each day.",
